About the author
----------------

Maurizio Cammalleri is a system architect based in Italy. In more than
thirty years of work, Maurizio has designed embedded systems, industrial
control software and high-performance web platforms, and has led technical
teams as CTO and CIO.

Janas is part of Prabanta (https://github.com/prabanta-dev), a family of
tools written in C with no dependencies beyond the C library:

  - Limba, the front end of the compiler for Luxia, an extended Pascal
    with the rigour of Ada (https://github.com/prabanta-dev/limba);
  - Meri, the register-based virtual machine that runs it, with a
    bytecode compiler, ahead-of-time and just-in-time compilation;
  - Janas, tools for artificial intelligence, whose aim is to lower the
    hardware that private AI needs (https://github.com/prabanta-dev/janas);
  - Napa, for web infrastructure, and Prama, for high-performance
    computing, both still being defined.

Other public projects by the same author, in Free Pascal:

  - SedaiBasic2, a BASIC interpreter with a virtual machine
    (https://github.com/camauri/SedaiBasic2);
  - Sedai Audio Foundation, an audio synthesis library with five engines:
    subtractive, FM, wavetable, additive and SID
    (https://github.com/camauri/SedaiAudio).

Writing: https://cammalleri.substack.com/


Others
------

The libraries of others compiled into some of the programs keep their
authors and their licences, beside their sources in src/third_party/
(GNU libmicrohttpd, yyjson, Mbed TLS). The one piece of data taken from
ggml, the IQ3_S grid in src/llm/iq3s_grid.h, is the ggml authors' under
the MIT licence.

What else Janas owes, and to whom - the models' authors, the reference it
is checked against, the people who tested it - is in the Credits section of
README.md.
